<!-- insight:spec-to-application -->
<h2>How kabuli is sized: counts per ounce and millimetre grades</h2>
<p>Kabuli is the large, cream-coloured, thin-skinned chickpea used whole: the base for hummus, canned chickpeas, salads and roasted snacks. It is sold on size, and size is stated in one of two ways.</p>
<p><strong>Count per ounce.</strong> A 42-44 count means roughly 42 to 44 chickpeas per ounce, so the individual chickpeas are large. A 58-60 count means smaller chickpeas and more of them per ounce. The lower the count, the larger the chickpea and the higher the price, because large, uniform kabuli is harder to grow consistently at scale.</p>
<p><strong>Millimetre grades.</strong> Kabuli is also graded over screens and sold as 7mm, 8mm, 9mm or 10mm+. The number is the screen size the chickpeas were retained on. It is a minimum, so a 9mm lot will contain some larger chickpeas.</p>
<p>Either basis works if the contract uses one consistently and states how it will be checked. Canners and hummus makers usually care most about uniformity, because mixed sizes cook unevenly. Retail whole-pack buyers care most about the visible size of the top grade.</p>
<p><strong>What the count means in grams.</strong> An ounce is 28.35 g, so the count converts directly to seed weight. A 42-44 count kabuli averages about 0.64-0.68 g per seed; a 58-60 count about 0.47-0.49 g. The larger grade's seed is roughly 38% heavier, and that difference decides the application. Retail whole packs, salads and premium canning want the large seed because the buyer sees it. Hummus and paste makers grind the chickpea, so they gain little from size and buy smaller counts on price, provided the lot is uniform enough to cook evenly. Scenario: a hummus plant moving from 58-60 to 42-44 pays for appearance it then grinds away; a retail packer making the opposite move saves money but sells a visibly smaller product.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>How desi is traded: dehusked, milled or left intact</h2>
<p>Desi chickpeas are smaller, darker (usually light to dark brown) and thicker-skinned, with a rougher surface. Most desi trade goes into processing. Dehusked and split, it becomes chana dal, a staple across South Asia. Milled, it becomes besan (gram flour), used for batters, snacks and sweets. Whole desi is also traded, for markets that cook it whole or sprout it.</p>
<p>Desi is not graded by count per ounce. Buyers specify form (whole or split), moisture, purity, foreign matter and, for dal, the level of broken pieces and residual husk. For millers splitting desi themselves, uniform size matters because it drives dehusking yield. Ask for a sample before committing to a new origin lot.</p>

<h2>Which tolerances should a chickpea contract write down?</h2>
<p>Type and size set the grade. These tolerances decide whether the lot you receive matches it:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Parameter</th><th scope="col">Typical export level</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Moisture</td><td>≤12-14%; ≤12% where long storage is planned</td><td>Higher moisture raises mould and insect risk in the container and in your warehouse</td></tr>
<tr><td>Foreign matter</td><td>≤1%</td><td>Stones, stalks and soil; a direct cleaning cost for canners and millers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Admixture / damaged</td><td>≤2-3%, per contract</td><td>Covers insect-damaged, shrivelled and other-type grains, like desi in a kabuli lot</td></tr>
<tr><td>Splits and broken (whole grades)</td><td>Stated as a maximum on the contract</td><td>Splits cook unevenly and look poor in a retail pack</td></tr>
<tr><td>Colour uniformity (kabuli)</td><td>Agreed against a reference sample</td><td>Off-colour and dark-spotted grains are what retail buyers notice first</td></tr>
<tr><td>Size / count</td><td>Kabuli: count per ounce or mm grade</td><td>The main price driver within kabuli</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>India's chickpea crop is a rabi (winter-sown) crop, harvested February to April. It is cleaned and size-graded after harvest and then shipped through the rest of the year. Kabuli is concentrated in Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra and Rajasthan, while desi grows across a broader belt of central and northern India.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>Chickpeas are a stored product, and many destinations want proof of fumigation against storage insects. The fumigation certificate usually goes alongside the phytosanitary certificate. The certificate of analysis should mirror your contract: moisture, foreign matter, admixture, splits and, for kabuli, the count per ounce or millimetre grade. Put the grade on the commercial invoice as well, so the paperwork and the goods can be checked against each other at the destination. Indian export policy for pulses is set in DGFT's ITC(HS) schedule, and we confirm the current position for HS 0713.20 when each order is placed.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<!-- insight:price-mechanism -->
<h2>What moves the price between chickpea grades</h2>
<p>Kabuli and desi are priced as separate markets, and the gap between them changes from season to season. Within kabuli, size is the main lever. Each step toward a lower count per ounce or a larger millimetre grade is a step up in price, because a smaller share of any crop grades out at the top sizes. Uniformity and colour add a premium on top of size. Within desi, form matters more: split chana dal carries processing cost and yield loss that whole desi doesn't.</p>
<p>Outside the specification, the Indian crop is the biggest driver. Rainfall and planted area in the major growing states, the February-April harvest, domestic demand (chickpeas are a staple in India) and any change in government policy on pulses all feed through to export offers. Crop size in other major producing countries and competing supply into your market also matter, as do exchange rates and freight to your port. The WITS figures show how much the market moves year to year: 2024 value per kilogram was well above 2023's.</p>
<p>The annual export series shows the scale. India's chickpea exports were worth USD 89.1 million in 2021, USD 225.0 million in 2022, USD 287.2 million in 2023 and USD 283.2 million in 2024 (WITS/UN Comtrade). Value grew about 2.5 times in a single year, from 2021 to 2022, then levelled off. Those are historical totals, not current prices, but they show that the export market for Indian chickpeas can expand or tighten quickly. A buyer on a fixed annual contract carries that swing on one side or the other, so many programmes price per shipment or per quarter instead.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your type, size grade, form, tolerances, packaging and destination port. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us all of them.</p>
