<!-- insight:rejection-patterns -->
<h2>What goes wrong when a chickpea grade is loosely specified</h2>
<p><strong>"Best quality" with no type or grade.</strong> Buyers new to chickpeas sometimes ask for "the best quality available" and expect the supplier to work out the right product. "Best" means large, uniform kabuli to a hummus maker and consistent desi with good dehusking yield to a dal processor. An open request gets an offer you can't compare. Start with the end use, even loosely defined, and the grade follows from it.</p>
<p><strong>Count range without a measuring method.</strong> Count grading has natural batch-to-batch spread even within a stated range. A 42-44 count checked on a hand-picked handful will look better than one counted on a properly drawn sample. Put the sampling and counting method in the contract, and agree which figure governs: your inspector's, ours, or a nominated third party's.</p>
<p><strong>Mixing count and millimetre offers.</strong> One supplier quotes 58-60 count and another 9mm, and the buyer compares the prices directly. They are not the same grade. Ask both for a sample, or ask both to quote on your chosen basis.</p>
<p><strong>Moisture that is fine at loading but not at arrival.</strong> Chickpeas loaded near the top of the moisture tolerance can sweat in a container crossing warm seas, leaving condensation damage in the top layer of bags. Ask for ≤12% on long voyages or long-storage programmes.</p>
<p><strong>Skipping the sample on a new grade.</strong> If you are stepping up from 58-60 to a premium 42-44, request a sample first. It costs days and avoids a dispute over a full container.</p>
<p><strong>Insects and residues found at the destination.</strong> Weevil and other storage-insect damage is the defect that grows after loading, which is why fumigation and an insect-damage limit both belong in the contract. Some destinations also test for pesticide residues against their own MRL (maximum residue limit) and require an import permit before arrival. Scenario: a 20ft of 58-60 kabuli passes inspection at loading, but fumigation was rushed to meet the vessel cut-off and live weevils are found when the container is opened at the destination. The importer faces re-fumigation at its own cost, or rejection. Ask your importer for the destination's permit and residue requirements before contract, and schedule fumigation to fit the sailing date.</p>
<!-- /insight -->
<!-- operator-detail:end -->

